        <div id="circuit-vs-whole" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Circuit-Level vs Whole-Home Monitoring
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whole-home monitoring measures the main service conductors and tells you the total. It is enough to see your baseline, the always-on load that runs at 3 a.m. when everyone is asleep, and the big spikes when the dryer, oven or air conditioner kicks in. For a lot of households, finding and cutting that overnight baseline is the single most valuable thing a monitor does, and whole-home data is all it takes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Circuit-level monitoring adds a small CT on each branch circuit you care about. Emporia's Vue 3 supports up to 16 circuit sensors alongside the mains, which is enough to cover every major 240V load and the busiest 120V circuits in most homes. The payoff is certainty: the furnace blower, the well pump, the water heater and the kitchen each have their own line, and you never have to wonder whether the app guessed correctly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limit of circuit-level data is that a circuit is not an appliance. A bedroom circuit might feed a TV, a lamp, a phone charger and a window air conditioner, and the monitor sees only their total. Dedicated circuits, the ones that feed a single big appliance, give clean answers. Shared general-purpose circuits give useful but blended ones. That is why pairing a circuit monitor with a few smart plugs works so well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prioritize sensors for loads that are large, hardwired or suspicious. Electric water heaters, heat pumps, central air, electric ranges, dryers, well pumps, EV chargers and sump pumps are the usual high-value targets. A sump pump circuit is a good example of data that pays off beyond the bill: a pump that starts cycling far more often than usual is telling you something about the pump, the check valve or the groundwater before the basement does.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your panel is crowded, has a subpanel, or uses tandem breakers, tell the electrician before the visit. Circuit sensors need physical room to clip around each conductor, and fitting 16 of them into an already packed panel is not always possible. In some cases monitoring the feeder to a subpanel with one pair of sensors is a smarter use of the hardware than chasing every individual circuit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="device-detection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Device Detection: What It Can and Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sense's headline feature is device detection. It samples the whole-home signal at very high speed, which Sense describes as a million times per second, and uses machine learning to recognize the electrical signatures of individual appliances as they turn on and off. When it works, an appliance appears in the app with its own usage history, with no sensor on its circuit at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            It takes time and it is not complete. Sense is upfront that detection happens gradually over weeks and months, and owners commonly report that big, simple loads such as water heaters, dryers, ovens, well pumps and older refrigerators are found first. Small electronics, devices with variable-speed motors, and anything that draws a similar signature to something else may never be identified individually. They stay lumped in an 'other' bucket.
          </p>

          <p>
            Variable-speed equipment is the hardest case. Inverter-driven heat pumps, mini-splits, modern variable-speed furnace blowers and some newer refrigerators ramp their power up and down smoothly instead of clicking on and off, which leaves no sharp signature to learn. If your biggest loads are of that kind, plan on dedicated sensors for them, through Sense's add-on options or a circuit-level monitor like Emporia, rather than waiting for detection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Detection also mislabels. It may decide two similar loads are one device, or name something incorrectly until you correct it. Treat the device list as a helpful starting point, not a meter-grade breakdown. For the questions that need exact numbers, like whether a heat pump is short-cycling or how many kilowatt-hours the EV charger used last month, a dedicated sensor on the circuit is the right tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes Sense a bad buy. For a household that wants insights without labeling 16 circuits, it surfaces patterns automatically and improves over time. It just should not be bought on the expectation that every device in the house will show up by name. If that expectation matters to you, circuit sensors or smart plugs are the honest answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Monitoring Solar With a Home Energy Monitor
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you have solar panels, a monitor that only watches the mains sees net flow: power coming in from the grid at night and power going out when the panels overproduce. It cannot tell you how much the array actually made or how much of that you used yourself. To separate production from consumption, the monitor needs its own sensors on the solar circuit, which is what the Sense Energy Monitor with Solar adds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Emporia handles solar by using additional sensors on the solar breaker, and Emporia's app can show production, consumption and net flow together once it is configured. Either way, the installer has to identify the right conductors, usually where the inverter output lands on a breaker in the panel or in a separate solar subpanel. That is another reason to have an electrician, ideally one who has seen your solar layout, do the install.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why bother when the inverter already has an app? Because the inverter app knows production but usually not consumption, and the utility knows net flow but often only hourly or daily. A whole-home monitor with solar sensors puts all three in one place in near real time. That is what lets you shift laundry, dishwashing and EV charging into the hours when the panels are producing, which matters more every year as utilities pay less for exported power.
          </p>

          <p>
            A smart meter reader like the EAGLE-3 can often show both import and export, since many smart meters record both directions. It still cannot see production on its own. If you already have an inverter app, combining it with a meter reader gets you most of the picture without panel work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are considering adding a home battery, solar monitoring becomes far more useful. Knowing how much excess production you export on a typical summer afternoon, and how much you pull from the grid after sunset, is exactly the data needed to judge how large a battery would actually fill and empty each day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backup-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Using the Data to Size a Battery Backup or Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where a home energy monitor quietly earns its price. Battery and generator sizing comes down to two numbers: the peak watts the loads you want to run draw at once, and the total kilowatt-hours they use over the outage you are planning for. Spec-sheet guesses are notoriously unreliable for both. A month of real data from your own house replaces them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with energy. Look at your daily kilowatt-hour totals and, if you have circuit or device data, the share used by the loads you would actually keep running in an outage: refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, well pump, sump pump, internet and a few lights. Those essentials are usually a small fraction of the whole-home total. That figure is what you need when comparing the capacity of the systems in our <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">home battery backup guide</a> or deciding whether a portable unit can carry you through a night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at peaks. A well pump, a furnace igniter or an air conditioner compressor draws a short starting surge well above its running watts. Circuit-level data at one-second resolution shows those spikes and how they overlap. A battery inverter or generator has to handle the largest combination that can start together, not just the average, and a monitor showing several big loads switching on at once tells you to stagger them or size up.
          </p>

          <p>
            For generators, the same logic applies to running and starting watts. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> walks through sizing, and your monitor's data makes it concrete. Whatever you choose, it must connect through a properly installed transfer switch or interlock fitted by a licensed electrician; see our <a href="/best-generator-transfer-switch/">transfer switch guide</a>. Never backfeed a house through a dryer outlet or a double-ended cord. It can energize the utility lines and kill a lineworker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monitoring also pairs naturally with protection. While the electrician is in the panel installing CT clamps, it is the ideal moment to discuss a whole-house surge protective device, which mounts at the same panel and guards the electronics your monitor is busy measuring. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector guide</a> covers the types and ratings, and doing both in one visit saves a second service call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smart-plugs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Smart Plugs: The Cheap Way to Track One Appliance
          </h2>

          <p>
            A smart plug with energy monitoring is the fastest way to answer a narrow question. Plug in the appliance, wait a week, and read off its daily kilowatt-hours. Multiply by your electricity rate and you know what it costs to run. It is the most useful tool there is for deciding whether an old refrigerator, a garage freezer or a dehumidifier is worth replacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rate matters more than people think. The Kasa KP115 is rated for 15A, which is the limit of a standard household receptacle and enough for most plug-in appliances. Space heaters, window air conditioners and portable heaters run close to that limit for hours at a time, which is hard on any plug, adapter or receptacle. If you monitor a heater, check the plug's rating, check it is fully seated, and do not plug the smart plug into a power strip or extension cord.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smart plugs also give you control: schedules, remote off, and in some apps automations that trigger on power draw. That is handy for lamps and electronics. Be careful about using it on appliances that should never be switched off remotely, like a refrigerator, freezer or medical equipment. A mistaken schedule can cost a freezer full of food.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a desk, an entertainment center or a network closet, a multi-outlet strip like the Kasa HS300 is more practical than a handful of single plugs. Each outlet reports its own consumption, so you can see what the game console draws in standby or how much the network gear costs to keep on. Those always-on loads add up over a year, and they are invisible on a whole-home graph.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good rule: use smart plugs for discovery and a panel monitor for the whole house. If you find one appliance using far more than it should, the plug has paid for itself. If you find yourself buying a dozen plugs to cover the house, a circuit-level panel monitor is probably the better purchase.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Home Energy Monitor to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want detailed, unambiguous data and you are paying an electrician anyway, buy the Emporia Vue 3 with circuit sensors. Hard per-circuit numbers answer more questions than any inference engine, and they are what you want for sizing backup power, tracking a heat pump or catching a failing pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want insights without labeling circuits, buy the Sense Energy Monitor. It suits people who want the app to tell them things, and who are fine with detection filling in over months. If you have solar, buy the Sense Energy Monitor with Solar instead, or plan solar sensors on an Emporia, since net-only data is misleading on a solar home.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you rent, cannot open the panel, or just want whole-home numbers with zero installation, check your utility first and then buy the Rainforest EAGLE-3. If your utility does not support it, a meter reader is not an option and you should not buy one hoping it will work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your question is about one or two appliances, skip the whole-home monitors and buy a Kasa KP115 or two. For a desk or entertainment center full of always-on gear, the Kasa HS300 strip is the better fit. Either one is a cheap, honest way to find out whether a home energy monitor is something you will actually use before spending more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing CT clamps yourself in a live panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The service conductors and main lugs stay energized even with the main breaker off, and only the utility can kill them. Clipping sensors around them and connecting a breaker is work for a licensed electrician, who will also know whether your area needs a permit and how to keep the panel compliant.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting device detection to name everything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whole-home detection finds big, simple loads first and may never identify electronics or variable-speed equipment like inverter heat pumps. If you need exact numbers for specific appliances, put a circuit sensor on them or use a smart plug instead of waiting months for an algorithm to guess.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a meter reader before checking the utility</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Smart meter readers only work if your meter is compatible and the utility supports and authorizes the pairing. Many utilities do not. Search your utility's site for its home area network or smart meter data program before you buy, or you may end up with a gateway that never connects.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Monitoring a solar home with mains sensors only</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mains sensors on a solar home see only net flow, which can look like low usage on sunny days. Without sensors on the solar circuit you cannot separate production from consumption. Buy a solar-capable configuration or the numbers will mislead you about both the array and the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Wi-Fi at the panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Breaker panels sit in basements, garages and exterior walls inside a metal box that blocks signal. A monitor that keeps dropping off Wi-Fi leaves gaps in exactly the data you bought it for. Check signal strength at the panel first and plan an access point or mesh node if it is weak.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

