        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are home energy monitors worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it when you use the data to change something: cutting an always-on baseline, replacing an inefficient appliance, shifting loads to solar hours, or sizing a battery or generator. Owners who only want a graph often stop checking within weeks. If you are unsure, start with an inexpensive energy-monitoring smart plug on the appliance you suspect and see whether the numbers change your behavior.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a Sense or Emporia Vue myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both makers recommend a licensed electrician, and that is the right call. Installation means removing the panel cover and working around service conductors that stay energized even with the main breaker off, then connecting the monitor to a breaker. An electrician will confirm the breaker type is listed for your panel, check permit requirements and make sure the cover closes properly afterward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between Sense and Emporia Vue?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sense watches the whole home and uses machine learning to detect individual appliances from their electrical signatures, which happens gradually and is never complete. The Emporia Vue measures circuits directly with up to 16 circuit sensors, so you get exact numbers per circuit but must label them yourself. Sense suits people who want automatic insights; Emporia suits people who want hard data.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do energy monitors work with solar panels?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do if they have sensors on the solar circuit. Without them, the monitor sees only net flow from the grid and cannot tell how much your array produced or how much of it you used. The Sense Energy Monitor with Solar includes the extra sensors, and the Emporia Vue can be configured with sensors on the solar breaker.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a home energy monitor help size a generator or battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and that is one of the best reasons to own one. A month of data shows your daily kilowatt-hours and the peak draw when big loads start together. Circuit-level data lets you total only the essentials you would run in an outage. Those two numbers, energy and peak power, are what battery and generator sizing actually depend on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How accurate are smart plug energy monitors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For estimating what an appliance costs to run, they are generally accurate enough, and they are far better than guessing from a nameplate rating. They are not utility-grade meters, so do not expect them to match a bill to the decimal. Measure for at least a week so the result covers cycling appliances like refrigerators and freezers through normal use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a smart meter reader work with my utility?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your meter is a compatible model and your utility runs a program that lets customers pair a device with it. Many utilities do; many do not. Look on your utility's website for a smart meter data, Green Button Connect or home area network program, or call customer service and ask whether devices like the Rainforest EAGLE-3 can be registered.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

